# IQTREE's ML search still has run-to-run floating-point-level nondeterminism even with
# a fixed seed and forced single-threading (see conf/modules.config) -- likely from
# non-associative summation order in its underlying numerical library (BLAS/SIMD), which
# no amount of seeding or thread-pinning controls from the outside. This cascades through
# every step numerically derived from its tree/model: the LOO-pruned per-sequence trees,
# EPA-ng's placements, and SATIVASCORE's scoring. Existence/naming is still checked via
# stable_path -- only content hashing is skipped here. Each of these steps' actual logic
# is covered deterministically by its own module-level tests, which use static fixtures
# (a fixed alignment+tree, or fixed jplace files) rather than a live IQTREE run.
iqtree/user-alignment.treefile
iqtree/user-alignment.iqtree
iqtree/user-alignment.log
sativaloosplit/**
epang/*
sativascore/user-alignment.mislabels.tsv
sativascore/user-alignment.summary.txt

# The final merged mislabels report (SATIVASCORE rows + any raxtax rows, see
# workflows/taxmarker.nf's collectFile) inherits the same nondeterminism, since it's
# built directly from sativascore/user-alignment.mislabels.tsv above.
mislabels/user-alignment.mislabels.tsv

# RAxML-NG's own .log echoes its full invocation, including the absolute staged-input
# paths under Nextflow's per-run work directory -- these differ between any two separate
# runs regardless of engine/Nextflow version, even though the tree/model outputs
# themselves (bestTree/bestModel/mlTrees/startTree) are reproducible (cpus = 1 pin, see
# conf/modules.config). Existence/naming still checked via stable_path.
raxmlng/user-alignment.raxml.log

# sativaepang/reference's refjson embeds its own build invocation
# (metadata.invocation_epac), absolute working-directory paths and all -- not
# byte-reproducible across separate runs even with identical scientific content.
sativaepang/user-alignment.refjson
